+Coordinate managed clients across instances, threads, and processes using a host-local lease. Lease acquisition by competing instances or processes must be nonblocking: if another owner holds the root lease, raise `ReshardBusyError` before performing the contending operation; clients can retry. In particular, normal cache operations and lifecycle operations must not wait for another native transaction to release the root lease. A native transaction owns the root lease until its outermost exit. Normal cache operations and nested `transact` calls by the same transaction owner remain reentrant; calls from different threads on one instance may be serialized. Do not silently drop operations on lease contention. Process death releases ownership; old instances refresh rather than continuing to use a retired topology. Native FanoutCache already implements `transact`: retain its ordinary successful-exit/exception rollback behavior, but do not claim stronger crash-atomic transactions spanning several independent SQLite databases or exactly-once external side effects.
 
 Durable topology and progress must survive abrupt process termination at arbitrary points in begin, copying, reconciliation, cutover, and abort. Before cutover, the source remains authoritative; recovery must replay incomplete work without losing later updates or duplicating entries. After a published cutover, reopen must use the complete destination and never roll back the epoch. Handle partial destination files safely. This task requires local process-crash recovery, not storage-device failure, power-loss guarantees, network filesystems, or malicious concurrent modification of cache internals.
 
